Prefer staged cloning to preflight/drop/create:

```sql
CREATE DATABASE exposed_search_regression_stage
  WITH TEMPLATE exposed OWNER exposed ALLOW_CONNECTIONS false;
```

Run through `docker compose exec -T postgres psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U exposed -d postgres`. Native cloning itself refuses an active source, preserving the existing target without relying on a racy preflight. PostgreSQL blocks new source connections during cloning. [CREATE DATABASE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createdatabase.html)

After successful staging, swap names in one transaction:

```sql
BEGIN;
-- Only when the target exists:
ALTER DATABASE exposed_search_regression
  RENAME TO exposed_search_regression_previous;
ALTER DATABASE exposed_search_regression_stage
  RENAME TO exposed_search_regression;
ALTER DATABASE exposed_search_regression ALLOW_CONNECTIONS true;
COMMIT;
```

Then `DROP DATABASE exposed_search_regression_previous` without `FORCE`. A busy target causes the rename to fail and the transaction to roll back. For absent targets, omit the first rename. Renaming permits transactions; the documented transaction restriction applies to changing tablespaces. [ALTER DATABASE](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-alterdatabase.html)

Keep fixed staging/previous names and refuse if either already exists, with a clear recovery message; never silently delete them. Bash needs `set -euo pipefail` and separate autocommit CREATE/DROP calls. A cleanup trap should only remove a stage created by this run, never the previous database.

Tradeoff: adds two reserved names, extra temporary disk space, and recovery handling. Preflight/drop/create is shorter but cannot guarantee preservation: source connections or cloning failures can occur after dropping the target. Staging is the strongest fit for the preservation requirement.
